# UI and UX

Audience is older adults and the people helping them. Clarity beats decoration.

## Readability

- Body text at least `1rem` (16px). Important labels and buttons at least `1.125rem`.
- Line height around `1.5`. Limit line length to about `70ch`.
- Main surface is white. Match the hero ad: deep teal-green (`#114d4a` / `#1f6a66`), soft mint surfaces, warm orange accent (`#d4572a`) sparingly. Cards stay white with dark text.
- Contrast at least 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text and icons. Do not rely on color alone.

## Layout and touch

- Touch targets at least 44×44px. Space tappable items so they are not adjacent.
- One primary action per view. Secondary actions are visually quieter.
- Stack on small screens. Use the existing content width (`max-width: 1200px`) and card width (`max-width: 700px`) unless a new layout needs more room.
- Prefer the existing type scale and radii (cards ~`30px`, controls ~`8px`) so new UI matches the coming-soon page.

## Interaction

- Every input has a visible `<label>`. Placeholder text is not a label.
- Buttons say what they do (`Search care`, `Call office`), not `Submit` or `Click here`.
- Show loading, empty, and error states in the page, with a way to retry.
- Honor `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. Keep motion short; do not loop decorative animation for users who opt out.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation: none !important;
    transition: none !important;
  }
}
```

## Accessibility

- Use semantic elements: `header`, `main`, `nav`, `button`, `label`. One `h1` per page; headings do not skip levels.
- Images need a useful `alt`. Decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Keyboard focus must stay visible. Do not remove outlines without a clear replacement.
- Icon-only controls need an accessible name (`aria-label`). Emoji are not the only label for an action.
